#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276} :activity/id #uuid "9b33318d-1bab-5993-91f6-d6c8c1d3d0a0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148743 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e276ddb-1c14-51fd-8261-8c02eb3294f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3803 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ae4dc56-971c-585e-ac6e-a1d1927975cc" :step/math-academy-id 21910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147341 :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400429}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "27bcbb90-63f9-5635-829b-a801f3809b3e" :step/math-academy-id 21917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147342 :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393126}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "0ba22f44-3353-5e3e-a64c-1e09fd59d6d9" :step/math-academy-id 21951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147343 :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "e4a1265b-c056-54ef-b3c5-eaaf48e49ec3" :step/math-academy-id 21954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148744 :tutorial/content "In general, given two planes \n\n$$\n{\\Pi_{{1}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{1}x+b_{1}y + c_{1}z+ d_1=0 \\qquad  \\text{and} \\qquad   {\\Pi_{{2}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{2}x+b_{2}y + c_{2}z+ d_2=0,\n$$\n\n we have the following cases, regarding the positions of their normal vectors  $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2 \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- *Case 1*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\nparallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ then ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$   and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ intersect on a straight line.\n\n- *Case 2*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\parallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ but the corresponding equations of the planes are not scalar multiples of each other, then the planes are distinct and are parallel to each other.\n\n- *Case 3*:  If the corresponding coefficients of ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ are all proportional, then the planes are coincident." :tutorial/id #uuid "50ffbdb5-c9d0-5815-9b29-b505cde7f0fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3804 :tutorial/title "Intersection of Two Planes in 3D"} :step/id #uuid "cba5c95d-7e83-5872-ae95-b6832ff486a2" :step/math-academy-id 21955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148745 :tutorial/content "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7303bab-ef2c-5a9b-9caa-dc6fcc7929d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3817 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "8aec20bb-3034-54e2-b58d-43a8fc04ec0d" :step/math-academy-id 22145}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147718}]}
